          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a current emotional and relational fact. The immediate qualification limits its extent rather than weakening its avowal force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.87,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal invokes Cordelia's existing filial bond and its duties toward Lear, rather than making a distinct new promise. Her subsequent explanation identifies those duties as obedience, love, and honour. Lear does not accept this as an adequate response to his demand.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is a measured, propositionally explicit avowal, carefully bounded by Cordelia's reference to her bond. The evidence does not present it as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.85,\"reason\":\"In addition to avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a deliberately limited answer that resists Lear's demand for competitive, expansive declarations. Its plainness rejects exaggeration without denying love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks Cordelia to declare how much she loves him in competition with her sisters and links the answer to her prospective inheritance. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives the focal avowal as a restrained statement of filial love, explicitly limiting it to the proper bond between daughter and father.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing daughter-to-father bond and the associated duties of obedience, love, and honour; she does not clearly make a new promise in the focal words. Lear responds by demanding that she amend her speech, so her bounded formulation is not accepted as an adequate answer to his request.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare's King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The scene supplies strong evidence for the avowal's linguistic force and for Cordelia's professed affection, including her asides. It does not independently establish every aspect of her inward state or the later fulfilment of the duties she invokes.\"}"
        }
      ],
